{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.74,"content_digest":"e09800581eefc6d36bdac8db6bdff1c611bf3c90a9ec4bddceddb771a1fb90fa","created_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:46+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[],"link_id":"foundation-link:31c12c78dfd672a11f251497","meta_principle_id":"meta:computer-science:reduction-completeness","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:mathematics:01M0HMBBMK2Y2926X0S7A8ZP70","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The equivalence between the two computational problems is a mutual reduction; the reduction-completeness principle provides the framework for transferring hardness and guarantees between them.","relation_type":"depends_on","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-22T04:12:58+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0KTQZ6M68SKHPKARZR7EKFP"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T03:59:46+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.78,"content_digest":"3b3d0f2ef31f7dfc56c1a54a979e1c8985860fb6d9e2bb726ac7b1fbbe51d0cd","created_at":"2026-08-23T07:17:29+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"bce8686b7b35dba13c1ff42742fbf33298dd66c58223246b06d44ff115418977","input_tokens":5756,"latency_ms":38309,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"d02735a16ceda376b538b22aa2dbedef505822927e5956be8dc42e25d8cdc81d","output_tokens":2514,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:70b660e919e6960c07633706","meta_principle_id":"meta:information-control-complexity:requisite-variety","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0PQNCZJ4SZSKTSMYVPE21TV","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The principle is a concrete case of requisite variety: a metric that takes the same value for real and randomized text has no variety with which to distinguish those two classes. The meta-principle's condition that 'only variety can absorb variety' directly explains why Zipf's and Heap's laws have no discriminative capacity.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T08:08:08+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0PTK9G6V6VXNVFWAWBHMSYT"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T07:17:29+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.97,"content_digest":"ec73f2a8d17809e8afc2454b500f77e9c00b6cbfd3cc25317fed68f162d18401","created_at":"2026-08-22T18:01:52+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"e217b2ede216eb7802ec4ac43dd3fb912f228578fe51c6b59bdcb9cd3d5caab3","input_tokens":6151,"latency_ms":26973,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"9eb9f622ada3d4f8fdea88ddebee287be5aebd1dc20b7fed9659d7b51463682f","output_tokens":2326,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:d1b0e09ed7f1fb8d2112ba85","meta_principle_id":"meta:mathematics-logic:fixed-point","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NA4Y3SY52RPF662EPK16XQ","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The LLM-RAN closed-loop convergence claim is a specialization of the contraction-mapping fixed-point theorem: the closed-loop operator F is a contraction on the state space, so the Banach fixed-point theorem supplies existence, uniqueness, and convergence of the state sequence to s*. The conditions (contraction under a suitable norm), variables (state sequence and fixed point), scale (any normed state space), and epistemic type (formal theorem) are compatible.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T18:01:52+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.72,"content_digest":"f01a6a5212ac59732e5c0d3cab02faffb3ec7ee9f65de239307c47762b0acc07","created_at":"2026-08-22T21:43:33+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"7674fb50edaadc67f0815f46730b201692b290594de47ca02b60e00da945162e","input_tokens":5909,"latency_ms":22435,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"b60d488a91bad6bddf9bd6b8a76bbc37a2d3192fa3a8df3c7c42eab0ec2923a0","output_tokens":2316,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:e545bf2768b8f2673d2de09d","meta_principle_id":"meta:economics-game-theory:creative-destruction-growth","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0NPTZPBXXRKGW58K9MF52K7","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"Creative destruction holds that innovation and growth depend on preserving entry and that market power or entry barriers can block future innovation. The principle applies this logic to generative AI: high capital requirements raise entry barriers, disproportionately exclude smaller actors, and thereby reduce diversity and innovation. The variables align at the industry level, and the epistemic types are compatible as an economic mechanism applied to a specific market context.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T02:58:57+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0P5WC937PV7PKQMASBZ4D3Z"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-22T21:43:33+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
